- 5. Clinical Application & Analysis: {"5-1. clinical_dataset": "ABIDE I; ABIDE II; ADNI; AIBL; DEMGEN; TOP", "5-2. diseases_studied": "Alzheimer's Disease (AD); Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD); Bipolar Disorder (BD); Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI); Schizophrenia (SZ)", "5-3. clinical_groups_n": "AD: 116, ASD: 317, BD: 238, MCI: 122, SZ: 195", "5-4. clinical_groups_age": "AD: 73.11 (7.60), ASD: 12.35 (4.42), BD: 31.61 (11.40), MCI: 67.25 (9.27), SZ: 28.29 (9.45)", "5-5. clinical_groups_sex": "AD: F 55%, M 45%; ASD: F 17%, M 83%; BD: F 55%, M 45%; MCI: F 42%, M 58%; SZ: F 41%, M 59%", "5-6. deviation_metric": "z-score; median, variance, and percentage of extreme deviations (|z| > 1.96)", "5-7. association_analysis": "Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost were used for prediction. The area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) was the primary performance metric. Feature importance was assessed based on Logistic Regression coefficients.", "5-8. key_findings_brief": "Cerebellar features offered moderate power for prediction of ASD and SZ, but did not reliably distinguish reference samples from patients with BD, MCI, or AD.", "5-9. key_findings_detailed": "The results show that lobular and voxelwise cerebellar data can be used to discriminate reference samples from individuals with ASD and SZ with moderate accuracy (the area under the receiver operating characteristic curves ranged from 0.56 to 0.65). For SZ, the most predictive models were those centered around median and variance measures summarized within ROIs for the voxelwise models. In contrast, for ASD, models based on the lobular volumes and voxelwise variance within ROIs were found to be the most predictive.", "5-10. key_limitations": "The study acknowledges several limitations, including the limited predictive power of the models, potential collinearity among features, the inherent heterogeneity of the clinical conditions, challenges in interpreting individual-level deviations, the presence of subgroups within disorders, and the appropriateness of using adult templates for younger populations.", "5-11. application_notes": "The study highlights the utility of cerebellar normative modeling in predicting ASD and SZ, aided by 4 cerebellar atlases that enhanced the interpretability of the findings."}
